WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.220
[Keep 'Em Out]

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:15.820
[Produced by Stark-Films in cooperation with
the U. S. Public Health Service]

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:25.240
Images of bombs falling and buildings exploding,
fire, and debris flying.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:34.580
[Narrator:] The war is on. The enemy is attacking
on American soil. Our armed forces will take

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:41.680
care of the human invaders, but there is another
enemy, a vile destroyer of property, homes,

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:47.950
and lives who we must fight in every American
community. And it's everybody's fight.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.430
Let us look more closely into the rubble left
by this explosion.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:59.430
There. There he is. Our enemy, the rat. He's
about as loathsome a creature as we know,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:05.990
the bottom word in anybody's language. Look
at that wicked countenance. Ooh. The mere

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.520
sight of it gives you gooseflesh.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:16.520
This natural abhorrence of rats is old as
man himself and for these reasons:

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.530
They do tremendous damage to our buildings,
gnawing

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:26.260
holes, weakening structures, ruining plumbing,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:33.260
and starting fires.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:39.280
They ruin large quantities of food and other
supplies, destroying ten times as much as

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.700
they eat, and polluting great quantities more
that might have been eaten by human beings.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.760
[Sign reading Quarantined By order Health
Dept.]

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.240
And most important, they carry diseases, including
the dread plague and typhus fever.

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:59.880
Sometimes the diseased germs are carried on
the rat's feet.

00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:10.219
But more often, the infection is spread by
secondary parasites like lice and fleas.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:15.760
The infected flea leaves a sick or dead rat,
attaches

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.710
himself to a live, healthy one, and is thus

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:25.710
taken into the rat's nest where fleas live
and breed abundantly.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:32.480
Infected fleas are then carried from the nest
into your home or the place you work or shop

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.640
or visit, where they are knocked off by the
rat's scratching.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:43.390
They hop around on the floor until time for
the next meal. You provide this next meal,

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:47.120
and you become infected through the break
in your skin where the bite occurs.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.160
It is the close association of rats and man
living together which constitutes a health

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.400
menace. Prevent this association, and you
break the chain of infection.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:05.240
The rat is by far our most costly mammalian
pest and the most dangerous animal enemy of

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:05.410
man.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.690
[Map of U.S. with figure of 150,000,000 next
to image of rat displayed atop it.]

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:12.360
In the United States, there are more rats
than human inhabitants. It costs approximately

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.900
two dollars a year to feed one rat, and the
estimated annual board bill in this country

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:24.900
alone is well over 300 million dollars, a
rather high figure for an unwelcome guest,

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.800
especially in times like these.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:35.120
You might well ask why do we pay for it? Public
health officials know how to get rid of rats

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.840
and the story can be told in three short lines.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.860
Block their entrance into buildings, eliminate
enclosed spaces where they nest, catch and kill.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:54.020
It is always better to know the enemy before
we begin to fight. By studying his habits

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:00.340
and needs, we discover the weak points of
his defense, and then we can strike where

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.040
it will do most good.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.020
For example, rats hug the wall when climbing
stairs. So we catch them with traps placed

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.150
near the wall.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:19.190
Rafters like these are no barrier
to a rat's progress, but on them, he leaves

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:24.740
his mark.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:34.220
We recognize the signs and place traps in
the way. We know that rats eat meat, grain,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:40.710
vegetables, garbage, almost anything, and
they will do seemingly impossible things in

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.780
order to get at food.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:50.780
Take this fellow, for instance, gnawing through
a lead pipe. And here is as good an exhibition

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:58.340
of wire walking as you've ever seen at the
circus.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:08.440
This rat knows that the wire leads to food.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:19.210
After he gets to the building, he must find
an opening into the house. The instinct to

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:25.390
nest and reproduce is as strong as the instinct
to eat. So rats naturally seek a home.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:30.240
Usually, they don't have to go to much trouble
to find homes because we provide these for

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.650
them, made to order, steam-heated. Everything
but the welcome mat.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:41.970
These are typical examples of conditions and
factors responsible for the presence of rats

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:45.450
in our buildings.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:50.240
And don't think they're necessarily slum-dwellers.
They will live in any building that provides

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.140
harborage, even homes like this.

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:02.520
We know that rats are very prolific, producing
litters of five to ten every couple of months.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.800
According to estimates of the United States
Fish and Wildlife Service, they are the most

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:13.800
prolific of all mammals, and a single pair
of rats left to reproduce unhampered would

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:21.180
become 1,500 in a year.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.900
And 360 million in the short space of three
years.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.220
Of course, they're not allowed to multiply
unhampered. So the situation is not quite

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:39.220
so bad. It's plenty bad enough, however, and
high time we took the offensive and counterattacked.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:50.360
Here are a few of the things you can do. Get
rid of old rubbish. Eliminate enclosed spaces

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:57.750
in which rats might nest - under platforms,
under stairs, under houses.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:06.330
Close openings around pipes and wires. Or
better still, make such openings to fit exactly

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:13.430
the pipes they admit, and put metal collars
around them to prevent breaks or gnawing.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.000
Rats will not burrow in the ground deeper
than 20 inches. So make the foundations of

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.210
your buildings rat-proof two feet below and
one foot above ground.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:29.480
Concrete or a metal sheet sunk along the building
wall will serve this purpose.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:36.440
Avoid double walls where possible, but if
you use them, be sure they are kept intact

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:42.449
so rats cannot run between them. Blocks and
jams help.

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:48.350
Cover basement windows and other openings
with wire mesh or perforated sheet steel.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:54.560
Any opening larger than half an inch will
admit rats. If you can get your forefinger

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.419
into it, a rat can squeeze through.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:05.169
Where wire mesh or glass is used, it must
be protected and kept intact. Once broken,

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:12.050
your protection is gone. Wherever possible,
use rat-proof materials: metal, concrete,

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:15.729
plaster, stone, bricks, or tile.

00:08:15.729 --> 00:08:22.729
This door is flashed with metal, and the door
pull keeps it closed when not in use.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:30.660
Concrete is the most nearly perfect material
for rat-proofing. Repair breaks in walls as

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.180
soon as they are discovered in order to keep
'em out.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:39.510
Buildings where food is kept must be unusually
well-protected, like this one with concrete

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:43.510
floor, brick walls, and tight-fitting metal
door.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:49.820
Finally, there should be periodic inspections
to see that buildings are properly maintained.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:59.740
Fight rats with knowledge. Ask your health
department for help. Rat control is up to you.

00:09:01.129 --> 00:09:07.059
While individual effort can do a lot, the
concerted effort of the whole community is

00:09:07.059 --> 00:09:10.730
needed to solve the problem completely.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:17.730
Eternal vigilance and a well-planned continuing
program should be our answer to this challenge.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:24.240
So long, rat. The outlook for you is not very
good.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.900
[ Music and image of plump rat.]

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:33.420
Keep them out!

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.600
[The End, Stark Films]

